        "narrative": "The data layer was deliberately re-examined: would SQLite earn its keep for drill storage, a search index, session logs, or roster history?\n\nVerdict: no — because the file layout IS the product's key feature. Every drill being a human-readable file means: (1) AI agents author drills directly on a subscription — the zero-API-cost route the whole tool is designed around; (2) git gives history, diffs, review and backup for free; (3) the coach can open any drill in a text editor; (4) a fresh clone needs nothing but npm ci. A binary store would subtract all four and add a native dependency (better-sqlite3 compiles per Node ABI) to provide indexes and transactions that a personal library measured in TENS of files cannot feel — in-memory search over library summaries is already instantaneous, and the heaviest read path (library previews) is bounded by SVG rendering, not file IO.\n\nWhat would change the verdict: full-text search across many hundreds of drills, concurrent multi-coach editing, or per-rep analytics. Even then, the right shape is SQLite as a REBUILDABLE INDEX over the files — a cache, never the truth — so the agent route survives intact. Until a concrete feature needs that, there is nothing for a database to do here but cost.",
