        "id": "pitch_geometry",
        "title": "Meters, origin, apron",
        "question": "Why can an agent place a striker at x:44 and have it just work?",
        "narrative": "The board's coordinate system is physical: 1 SVG user unit = 1 meter, origin at the pitch's top-left corner, x along the length (0 = left goal line), y across the width, with a 3m apron of legal out-of-bounds staging space (so x:-2 is 'just off the end' — useful for subs and equipment).\n\nEvery pitch format is one PitchSpec entry: 11v11 (100×64, full IFAB-ish markings with penalty arcs and corner arcs), 9v9 (70×50), 8v8 (60×40), half-pitch (one end + open halfway), and a resizable training grid. One renderer (PitchMarkings) reads the spec and mirrors end features, so adding a format is adding a table row. Token glyphs are sized in meters too, with a per-format scale nudge so a 9v9 board doesn't show comically large players.\n\nThis is what keeps the agent docs honest: drill-authoring.md can say 'positions are meters; the 9v9 pitch is 70×50' and an agent's mental model matches the renderer exactly. The validator backs it up by flagging out-of-pitch or pixel-scale coordinates, and switching a drill to a smaller format pulls stranded pieces back inside the new bounds.",
